    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:contentBlock_text:579516a01eaca9ec",
      "field": "contentBlock.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "4ebcde2e24f24cc08a0029c2ed39e807977b7f7920cd352999ec9023e7bf469d",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-mw6lzct9-a8m9x2yez",
        "model": "gpt-6.1-sol"
      },
      "text": "Breakfast. Your guide will arrange the meeting time. An English-speaking escort will meet you in the lobby and take you to the railway station. The Expedition train takes you to Aguas Calientes, the village at the foot of Machu Picchu, also known as Machu Picchu Pueblo.\n\nAt Machu Picchu, you’ll walk among stone terraces, temples and ceremonial squares. The name means “Old Mountain” in Quechua. The city was built in the 15th century on a mountain ridge in the Andes. Its remote location helped preserve its buildings during the Spanish conquest, when many other Inca settlements were destroyed.\n\nIn 1911, Hiram Bingham’s expedition brought Machu Picchu to international attention. Local people had known about it long before then. On the tour, your guide will explain the city’s history, architecture and engineering. Machu Picchu has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1983 and one of the New Seven Wonders of the World since 2007.\n\nHave lunch at a restaurant, then return to the station and take the train to Cusco. Transfer to the 4-star Xima Cusco or a similar hotel. Overnight in Cusco."
    },
    {
